In a discussion with James regarding the review of direct climate impacts for the U.S. National Climate Assessment, a collection of relevant research findings was presented. Key insights included mixed effects of CO2-fertilization on crops (McLachlan et al., 2020) and significant economic damages projected to reach USD 2.9 trillion annually by 2090, primarily due to climate-driven health impacts (Hartin et al., 2023). Additionally, rising income inequality could exacerbate disaster effects (Cappelli et al., 2021), while changes in tropical cyclone impacts could increase the social cost of carbon significantly (Krichene et al., 2023). Climate change threats also extend to water resources, biodiversity, and health, with increasing extreme weather events projected to heighten vulnerabilities across multiple systems. The discussion emphasized the urgent need for comprehensive adaptation and mitigation strategies to address these intertwined impacts.
